; operator-algebra.flatrules — DRAFT
;
; Algebraic properties of FlatPIR operators, consumed by the term-rewriting layer
; alongside the equivalence rules (core-equivalences.flatrules). A declared property
; lets the matching rules be written ONCE per operand order: the engine realizes it
; its own way — canonical operand ordering for a greedy / canonical-form pass; native
; AC(I) matching or a controlled birewrite for an e-graph (egglog). Same notation as
; the rules (flatppl-design §12); the leading & marks a DSL keyword that frames
; FlatPIR heads, not FlatPIR itself.
;
; A bare marker means "match this head modulo the property, for all operands". A
; property that holds only on some operand TYPES is not a bare marker — it is a
; guarded &equiv (see mul, below).

; --- commutative:  f(a, b) = f(b, a) ----------------------------------------------
; Exact for every listed head, IEEE floating point included (a+b and a*b commute;
; only NaN payloads, left unspecified, may differ). superpose is measure addition,
; a commutative monoid; joint / cartprod are NOT here — their variate layout is an
; ordered cat (§06).
(&commutative superpose add min max land lor lxor equal unequal)

; --- associative:  f(f(a, b), c) = f(a, f(b, c)) ----------------------------------
; add and mul are deliberately ABSENT: real addition / multiplication are associative
; over ℝ but NOT in IEEE arithmetic (reassociation changes rounding), so declaring
; them would be unsound for numeric codegen. Integer add/mul are associative, but a
; per-head marker can't carry that type condition; an engine wanting integer
; reassociation applies it under a type guard instead.
(&associative superpose min max land lor lxor)

; --- idempotent:  f(…, a, …, a, …) = f(…, a, …) -----------------------------------
; Reserved for a future set(…) constructor (a set is commutative + associative +
; idempotent — ACI).
; (&idempotent set)

; --- conditional commutativity: mul -----------------------------------------------
; mul commutes for SCALARS only — matrix·matrix and matrix·vector do not — so it is a
; guarded equivalence, not a bare marker. The shared ?k ties both operands to the
; same scalar type via their inferred %meta (a guard reads inferred metadata, §11,
; whether or not the term carries a serialized wrapper). Rarer to fire than the bare
; markers, so a guarded birewrite is an acceptable cost.
(&termrules
  (&equiv (mul ?a ?b) (mul ?b ?a)
    (?= ?k (%scalar (?| real integer complex)))
    (?= ?a (%meta (?k ?? ??) ?_))
    (?= ?b (%meta (?k ?? ??) ?_))))
